X-SOURCE:Santa Rosa Junior College
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261018T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261018T171500
LOCATION:Student Center - Petaluma Campus
SUMMARY:Marking the Flesh: Revealing the Tattoo Practices of the Nile Val
 ley
DESCRIPTION:The North Coast Chapter of the Archaeological Institute of Am
 erica invites you to the following lecture:  Marking the Flesh: Revealing
  the Tattoo Practices of the Nile ValleyAbstract: For more than a century
 \, archaeologists knew that some ancient Egyptians and Nubians had tattoo
 s—but only a few tattooed people had been identified across Egypt and N
 ubia's vast ancient history.  Over the past decade\, however\, new techno
 logy and growing interest have dramatically changed our evidence for and 
 understanding of tattooing. This lecture will share the stories of these 
 tattooed people and the remarkable legacy of tattoo practices they repres
 ent—a tradition that began over 5\,000 years ago and continues today.*D
 r. Anne Austin is an Associate Professor of History at the University of 
 Missouri St. Louis (UMSL). Her research combines the fields of osteology 
 and Egyptology in order to document medicine and disease in the past.  Ev
 ent Details:  • Sunday\, October 18\, 2026\, SRJC Petaluma Student Cent
 er (https://maps.santarosa.edu/student-center)\, 4:00-5:15 p.m.  • This
  lecture is free and open to the public  • *This presentation may conta
 in images of human remains.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  The North Coast Chapter of the Archaeol
 ogical Institute of America invites you to the following lecture:</p><p> 
  Marking the Flesh: Revealing the Tattoo Practices of the Nile Valley</p>
 <p><strong>Abstract:</strong> For more than a century\, archaeologists kn
 ew that some ancient Egyptians and Nubians had tattoos—but only a few t
 attooed people had been identified across Egypt and Nubia's vast ancient 
 history.</p><p>  Over the past decade\, however\, new technology and grow
 ing interest have dramatically changed our evidence for and understanding
  of tattooing. This lecture will share the stories of these tattooed peop
 le and the remarkable legacy of tattoo practices they represent—a tradi
 tion that began over 5\,000 years ago and continues today.*</p><p><strong
 >Dr. Anne Austin</strong> is an Associate Professor of History at the Uni
 versity of Missouri St. Louis (UMSL). Her research combines the fields of
  osteology and Egyptology in order to document medicine and disease in th
 e past.</p><p>  Event Details:<br />  • Sunday\, October 18\, 2026\, <a
  href="https://maps.santarosa.edu/student-center" target="_blank" rel="no
 opener">SRJC Petaluma Student Center</a>\, 4:00-5:15 p.m.<br />  • This
  lecture is free and open to the public<br />  • *This presentation may
  contain images of human remains.</p>
